/**

@page page_peano4_particles_mesh_consistency Particle sorting and mesh consistency

\tableofcontents

A position or search-radius update can invalidate a particle's vertex and
level association. The update action sets under
peano4.particles.AbstractUpdateParticleGridAssociation restore the
association over subsequent mesh sweeps.

# Sorting sequence

The first sorting sweep detaches a moved particle and reassigns it spatially.
It can lift the particle to a coarser level where the destination is reachable.
A following sweep drops it through refined children until its search radius
matches the selected level.

Rank-boundary movement adds one exchange step. The source rank sends the
particle after its sweep, and the target rank receives and continues the drop
in the next sweep. A particle that cannot reach a local destination enters the
sieve and is inserted from there on a later sweep.

Every algorithmic step that changes positions or search radii therefore also
installs the sorting action set, and at least one following step continues the
sort. The application keeps sorting while `registeredAnyResorting()` or
`hasParticlesToBeSievedIntoVertices()` reports pending movement.

# Safe update point

`touchVertexLastTime()` is the preferred position update point because all
cell work involving the current vertex has finished. The parallel state and
search radius remain stable during the sweep, and the sorting action observes
the new position immediately afterwards.

A particle can be reassigned to a vertex whose last-touch event is still
pending. The particle's `MoveState` prevents a second physical update in that
sweep. Application kernels update only particles whose parallel state is
`Local`; virtual copies follow from the boundary merge.

# Dynamic mesh changes

Regrid migration carries vertex particle sets with their pool slots when rank
ownership changes. Erasing fine cells lifts affected associations towards
surviving levels. New refined levels do not force an immediate drop; the next
sorting sequence moves eligible particles onto them.

# Tunnelling

Tunnelling means a particle travels farther than the cell range represented by
its current association. The sorting algorithm lifts it until the displacement
is local on a coarser level, moves it there, and drops it again. Applications
can enable diagnostics when such movement violates their numerical model.

# Sieve

Each particle type owns a peano4::particles::SieveParticles instance. It
holds particles that cannot be inserted into a local vertex slot during the
current sweep. A subsequent sorting sweep bucket-sorts the sieve records into
the available rank-local mesh.

The collective sieve exchange concatenates the records submitted by all ranks.
It does not deduplicate physical particle identities.

Large sieve populations add global sorting and exchange work. Frequent sieve
use indicates displacements, rank crossings, or associations that the ordinary
lift-and-drop sequence cannot settle locally.

# Parallel execution

Particle containers reject subtree-task replication, so a particle step traverses
each rank serially. MPI rank boundaries still use local and virtual particle
states and the generated `InterRank` merge. No intra-rank particle merge is
issued.

*/
